{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>This twenty [years], etc.</b> The reason he said twenty years is that for a short period of time a thief and a swindler will guard himself from doing evil, but not over a long stretch of time. Therefore he said <b>these twenty years</b>, and all of them were equally for the good. And this matter is not to be taken from it as proof if Jacob had been with a man who was insensible to fraud — therefore he said <b>with you</b>: have you seen in me any injustice all your days? And he adduced conclusive proofs, as I shall explain with the help of Heaven:" "<b>Your ewes and your goats, etc.</b> One needs to know Jacob's intention in this. If it is to recount the details of his success — this is not the place for these things. Also his saying <b>and the rams of your flock</b>, etc. — who would let him eat them? And if a person did not know, until now who will testify to the matter?" "<b>Rather,</b> Jacob's intention was as follows: the way of a thieving shepherd is to be cunning and steal by claiming that not all the flock gave birth — some gave birth and some miscarried — and he takes for himself. From this point on, the great proof that Jacob stated is <b>your ewes and your goats did not miscarry</b>, and in this there is no room to suspect that 'I ate,' etc. — for if he had wanted to eat from his [flock], he would have had the opportunity to tell him that so-and-so many miscarried from the flock. And it was from HaShem that this came about, to guard His pious one from suspicion, and not a single one of his flock miscarried, so that he would not suspect him of having eaten it." "And the reason he said <b>the rams of your flock</b>: the Torah expressed a young goat in the language of 'ayil,' to teach from here that a one-day-old kid is called 'ayil.' Or perhaps he intends according to what the Rambam wrote in the Laws of Robbery (Laws of Robbery and Lost Property 2:14), and this is his language: 'One who steals a lamb and it becomes a ram,' etc. — this constitutes a change in his possession and he acquires it even though the owners had not despaired of it — end quote. Therefore he intended to say that even in a manner where he could not remove it from his possession according to the law, he did not do so — meaning, he did not steal from him a kid and raise it with him until it became a ram, and when he returned it he returned a kid, so that it turns out he ate what had grown and become a ram."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:29.819697309Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}